=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
There is no network fault. A thorough inspection of every layer — data-plane
reachability, kernel forwarding tables, RIP control-plane state, interface
addresses, and daemon health — shows the CS-lab-to-server-room path is fully
operational.

Affected component: none. All subsystems are behaving correctly.

Mechanism: RIP is converged across all three routers. Router0 holds
1.0.0.0/8 via 10.10.0.2 (router1, metric 3); router1 holds it via
20.20.0.2 (router2, metric 2); router2 has it directly connected on eth0
(1.0.0.1/8). Every prefix in the topology is present in every routing table.
All three ripd processes are running under watchfrr with correct network
statements and no inbound or outbound filters. All WAN and LAN interfaces
are UP with their expected IP addresses.

Localization: tested cs_lab1, cs_lab2, and cs_hod individually to pc1
(1.0.0.5) — 0% packet loss in all cases. A reverse probe from pc1 back to
cs_lab1 also succeeded. A full traceroute confirmed the 4-hop path
cs_lab1 → router0 → router1 → router2 → pc1 with no gaps.

Root cause: there is no fault; the user's report is a false premise. The
claim that "the router has forgotten the Server Room route" does not match
reality — route 1.0.0.0/8 is installed and active on router0 right now.

Corrective action: none required. The engineer should communicate to the
CS-lab users that connectivity to the server room is healthy and ask them
to recheck their client-side application configuration, authentication
credentials, or file-share mount points, which are the more likely
explanation for any access difficulty they experienced.
=== END ===
